-- Double-entry bookkeeping, the way accountants have kept the books
-- for five hundred years - now with the rules enforced by the runtime
-- instead of by hope and review.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case accounting is not your world.
-- Every transaction a business makes is written down as a "journal
-- entry" built from two or more lines. Each line records money moving
-- into an account (a debit) or out of one (a credit). The iron rule:
-- inside a single entry, the debits and the credits must add up to the
-- same total. Buy a 100-pound laptop with cash and you debit Equipment
-- 100 and credit Cash 100 - the books stay balanced. An entry whose
-- debits and credits disagree is not a "slightly wrong" entry; it is
-- not an entry at all.
--
-- Accountants also work in periods - a month, a quarter, a year. Once
-- a period is closed and reported (to a tax authority, a board, an
-- auditor), you must not quietly slip new entries back into it. A real
-- mistake in a closed period is fixed by *restating*: you post a fresh,
-- corrected entry and record that it supersedes the old one. The
-- original is left exactly as it was filed, because "what did we report
-- at the time?" must always have an honest answer.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about all this. It does not store
-- "facts" - it stores *claims* that it has agreed to admit because they
-- obey the rules. Two kinds of rule do the work below:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one. This is how we make a broken state impossible to save.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action - a condition checked at
--     the moment you act, and never again. The difference matters: the
--     balance rule is an invariant (the books must always balance), but
--     "you can't post into a closed period" is a gate. Closing a period
--     must not reach back and invalidate the entries that were posted
--     legitimately while it was open. An invariant governs what is
--     true; a gate governs what you may do next.

program double_entry_ledger

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold, and the shape of its arguments. These are the only nouns the
-- system knows: entries and their lines, which periods are closed,
-- which entry supersedes which, and the trial balance we read back out.
predicate JournalEntry(entry_id: Subject, posting_date: Subject, period: Subject)
predicate JournalLine(entry_id: Subject, account: Subject, debit_amount: Decimal, credit_amount: Decimal)
predicate PeriodClosed(period: Subject)
predicate Supersedes(new_entry_id: Subject, prior_entry_id: Subject)
predicate TrialBalanceRow(account: Subject, balance: Decimal)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - "an entry was posted", "a period was closed". They
-- are how a ledger tells the rest of the business what just happened.
intent JournalEntryPosted(entry_id: Subject)
intent PeriodClosed(period: Subject)
intent JournalEntryRestated(new_entry_id: Subject, prior_entry_id: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. If any change below would leave the
-- world violating one of these, the whole change is rejected.

-- The five-hundred-year-old rule itself: for every entry, its debits
-- and its credits sum to the same amount. Read `implies` as "whenever
-- the left is true, the right must be too".
invariant balanced_posted_entry:
    JournalEntry(entry, _, _) implies sum(d | JournalLine(entry, _, d, _)) = sum(c | JournalLine(entry, _, _, c))

-- An entry with no lines is meaningless paperwork. Every entry must
-- have at least one line.
invariant journal_entry_has_lines:
    JournalEntry(entry, _, _) implies (exists line: JournalLine(entry, line, _, _))

-- A correction history must stay a clean chain, not a fork: an entry
-- can be superseded by at most one successor.
invariant at_most_one_direct_successor:
    Supersedes(new_a, old) and Supersedes(new_b, old) implies new_a = new_b


-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still
-- holds once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim.

-- The everyday case: one account debited, one credited, same amount.
-- The gate allows posting only into an open period; the two equal-and-
-- opposite lines mean the balance invariant is satisfied by construction.
transformation post_simple_entry(
    entry_id, posting_date, period,
    debit_account, credit_account, amount
):
    require not PeriodClosed(period)
    admit JournalEntry(entry_id, posting_date, period)
    admit JournalLine(entry_id, debit_account, amount, 0)
    admit JournalLine(entry_id, credit_account, 0, amount)
    emit JournalEntryPosted(entry_id)

-- Real transactions are often one debit against several credits (a
-- salary split into pay, tax, and pension, say). The single debit must
-- equal the sum of the credits - and notice we do not have to check
-- that here: the balance invariant already guarantees it, and any
-- unbalanced split is simply refused at commit.
transformation post_split_entry(
    entry_id, posting_date, period,
    debit_account, debit_amount,
    credit_a_account, credit_a_amount,
    credit_b_account, credit_b_amount
):
    require not PeriodClosed(period)
    admit JournalEntry(entry_id, posting_date, period)
    admit JournalLine(entry_id, debit_account, debit_amount, 0)
    admit JournalLine(entry_id, credit_a_account, 0, credit_a_amount)
    admit JournalLine(entry_id, credit_b_account, 0, credit_b_amount)
    emit JournalEntryPosted(entry_id)

-- Close a period. After this, the gate in the posting transformations
-- turns away any new entry dated in that period.
transformation close_period(period):
    require not PeriodClosed(period)
    admit PeriodClosed(period)
    emit PeriodClosed(period)

-- Fix a mistake the honest way. Instead of editing the original entry
-- (which would erase what was reported), we post a corrected entry and
-- record that it supersedes the old one. The gate refuses to supersede
-- an entry that has already been superseded, so the correction chain
-- never forks. The original entry stays untouched, on the record.
transformation restate_entry(
    new_entry_id, prior_entry_id, posting_date, period,
    debit_account, credit_account, amount
):
    require JournalEntry(prior_entry_id, _, period)
    require not Supersedes(_, prior_entry_id)
    admit JournalEntry(new_entry_id, posting_date, period)
    admit JournalLine(new_entry_id, debit_account, amount, 0)
    admit JournalLine(new_entry_id, credit_account, 0, amount)
    admit Supersedes(new_entry_id, prior_entry_id)
    emit JournalEntryRestated(new_entry_id, prior_entry_id)


-- A trial balance lists every account and its net balance (total
-- debits minus total credits). Here it is a *derived* claim: a read-
-- only view computed straight from the journal lines whenever someone
-- asks for it, rather than a number stored on the side. Because it is
-- recomputed from the lines, it can never quietly drift away from them
-- - the reconciliation bug that haunts real accounting systems simply
-- cannot occur. One row per account; balance is debits minus credits.
derived TrialBalanceRow(account):
    over JournalLine(_, account, _, _)
    value balance = sum(d | JournalLine(_, account, d, _)) - sum(c | JournalLine(_, account, _, c))
